00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.800
When you think of kombucha,&nbsp;
you might think of a nice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.803
refreshing, healthy drink, one that’s&nbsp;
exceptionally good for your microbiome.

00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:27.440
But what we here at Journey to the 
Microcosmos&nbsp;think of is different from that.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.680
Our first thought is of a terrarium…

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.883
a place where a whole ecosystem&nbsp;
can exist, trapped in glass.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.107
Kombucha is an old and mysterious drink.

00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:56.960
It's&nbsp;thought to have originated 
around 221 BCE in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.120
Korea and China, though we had a hard time&nbsp;
tracking down the sources for that date.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.880
There are also reports of kombucha being&nbsp;
consumed in Russia in the 19th century.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.920
But those two time points don’t tell&nbsp;
us much about how kombucha came to be.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.200
And that’s not unusual when&nbsp;
it comes to ancient foods.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.920
Here on Journey to the Microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.840
we’ve ventured into the microbial world&nbsp;
of food like bread, yogurt, and cheese.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.480
And the processes humans have used to&nbsp;
make these foods go so far back and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.560
accompanied by so much lore that it is
difficult to untangle exact histories.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.360
But no matter how murky the history is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.200
the microbial universe buried in&nbsp;
these foods is somewhat consistent.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.760
There is an organism, maybe a&nbsp;
yeast or a bacteria or a fungus.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.040
It wants to survive, and so it&nbsp;
uses the resources around it—

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.880
like sugar or the proteins in milk—

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.182
to do that.

00:01:55.182 --> 00:01:59.840
 It might even work with one or&nbsp;
two other organisms to accomplish its goals.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.760
So James, our Master of Microscopes, thought&nbsp;
kombucha was going to be a very familiar sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.080
something chill like brewers yeast.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.000
Or something like the feeling he gets&nbsp;
when looking at fermentation products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.600
like yogurt under the microscope, which to him&nbsp;
resemble domesticated animals in a petting zoo.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.520
There’s a stable environment and plenty of food.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.040
And the organisms within have&nbsp;
no predators and no competitors.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.916
But that's not what he saw 
when he looked at kombucha.

00:02:28.916 --> 00:02:32.920
The word that came 
to mind then was “wild.”

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:38.000
It was so wild that at first he’d thought he&nbsp;
was looking at kombucha that had maybe gone bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.720
but he quickly realized that 
this was not the case.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.780
Kombucha is just wild like this.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.000
Now if you know anything about how to make&nbsp;
kombucha, then you’ve probably heard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.808
this weird thing that James is pulling&nbsp;
out of the top of his kombucha sample.

00:02:52.808 --> 00:02:54.880
This is the SCOBY.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:01.240
It’s a strange word, but it stands for&nbsp;something
“Symbiotic Culture Of Bacteria and Yeast”.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.480
So yes, that wiggly mass is actually the weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.040
wild world of bacteria and yeast&nbsp;
that makes kombucha possible.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.440
We’re not in the business of giving&nbsp;
recipes here on Journey to the Microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.280
and besides, everyone has 
their own preferences.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.000
So we’re just going to describe how&nbsp;
one might go about making a SCOBY.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.640
The process starts with some brewed tea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.480
usually black or green, which&nbsp;
is then mixed with some sugar.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.040
A bit of some prior kombucha is added to the mix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.520
providing a starter culture&nbsp;
of the bacteria and yeast.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.440
Then the container is covered with&nbsp;
something breathable and set aside.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.680
Over the next few weeks, bubbles begin&nbsp;
to form on the surface of the mixture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.560
gathering together until they form a thin layer.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.680
That layer eventually&nbsp;
thickens into the solid SCOBY.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.520
While the liquid used in this fermentation&nbsp;
process will ultimately be too vinegar-y to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:00.509
drink, the SCOBY can be used in subsequent&nbsp;
fermentations with brewed tea and sugar.

00:04:00.509 --> 00:04:05.760
For those of you who are experienced kombucha&nbsp;
brewers, you might be filling in the gaps of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.600
description with the myriad of choices that&nbsp;
make your kombucha taste the way it tastes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.760
whether that’s the use of store-bought cultures&nbsp;
or the addition of flavorings in later steps.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.000
That’s the thing about kombucha:

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.640
it is all so complicated, and again, wild.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:27.480
The community of bacteria and yeast that&nbsp;
makes up the SCOBY can vary tremendously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.280
depending on where the&nbsp;
kombucha was originally made.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:37.040
And as fermentation progresses, the composition of&nbsp;
the community changes as well, evolving with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.160
culture and responding to microbes in the air&nbsp;
that might try to interact with the kombucha.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:48.640
So this community we’re looking at might&nbsp;
have looked very different a few weeks prior.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.360
And the species might not be the exact same&nbsp;
species that make your favorite kombucha.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.840
But the underlying processes they engage&nbsp;
in to make that kombucha are the same.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.480
When the starter culture is mixed&nbsp;
with the brewed tea and sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:10.120
the yeast use an enzyme called invertase to&nbsp;
break down the sugar into smaller monomers.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.160
This kicks off a whole series of&nbsp;
metabolic reactions, beginning with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.920
the yeast and bacteria converting those sugar&nbsp;
monomers into ethanol and carbon dioxide.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.840
The ethanol could be a problem, as too much&nbsp;
can be toxic for the residents of the mixture.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:32.160
Luckily, the bacteria are able to oxidize the&nbsp;
ethanol, producing acetic acid in the process.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.153
At the same time, the bacteria makes&nbsp;cellulose, 
which helps form the biofilm of the SCOBY.

00:05:38.153 --> 00:05:44.120
These interactions demonstrate quite a bit of&nbsp;
cooperation between the bacteria and yeast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.520
and each carries out reactions that&nbsp;
make each other’s survival possible.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.480
They might break down sugar or lower&nbsp;
ethanol levels or create the molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.040
that will help bind the community going forward.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:01.960
These are all examples of cooperation,&nbsp;
and a nice foundation for an ecosystem.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.760
For a drink that is as associated with&nbsp;
wholesomeness as kombucha often is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:08.440
that is a nice image.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.520
But ecosystems aren’t marked&nbsp;
solely by cooperation.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.080
They’re also marked by competition, as&nbsp;
organisms vie for limited resources and space.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.400
There are several ways we might see this&nbsp;
competition taking place in kombucha.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.240
For example, not all yeast can&nbsp;
make invertase, that enzyme that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.840
kicks everything off by breaking down&nbsp;
sugar for the community to partake in.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.240
These non-invertase yeast are considered&nbsp;
“cheaters,” taking advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.320
resources provided by other organisms&nbsp;
without contributing any of their own.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:45.880
But one study of yeast grown with the&nbsp;
bacteria Escherichia coli found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:51.440
cheater yeast tend to not grow as&nbsp;
well as the yeast with invertase.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.600
In an environment where everyone wants sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.680
these cheater yeast don’t seem to survive&nbsp;
as well, perhaps because their non-cheating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.000
counterparts are able to consume a tiny&nbsp;
bit of the sugar monomers they produce.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.720
And that competition between yeasts&nbsp;
might carry over to the kombucha.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:12.520
Earlier, we mentioned that bacteria&nbsp;
oxidize ethanol into acetic acid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.640
lowering the pH of the environment overall.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:21.120
This acid is actually one of several acids found&nbsp;
in kombucha, leading scientists to wonder whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.240
acid in general serves as a protective quality&nbsp;
of the environment, deterring organisms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:33.920
can’t survive acidic conditions and creating a&nbsp;
gate against invaders that’s made out of that low pH.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.440
And there’s another potential shield&nbsp;
against invaders: the SCOBY itself.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:43.680
The thickness of the biofilm acts as a&nbsp;
physical deterrent, further strengthened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:49.615
by its composition, which makes it hard for other&nbsp;
organisms to diffuse their way into the kombucha.

00:07:49.615 --> 00:07:52.920
Now we should note that these&nbsp;
deterrents aren’t always effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.680
and sometimes unwelcome organisms&nbsp;
can find their way into the SCOBY—

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.360
at which point, you should&nbsp;
probably not drink that kombucha.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.600
It is this complex array of interactions, some&nbsp;
of which are better understood than others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.560
that makes us think of kombucha like a terrarium—

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.760
a glass bottle full of an environment you&nbsp;
can watch brewing itself an ecosystem.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.960
With every bit of SCOBY that bubbles to&nbsp;
the surface and attaches to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.360
we get to see this untamed landscape assemble.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:32.449
And with every SCOBY transferred to a new culture,&nbsp;
we can replicate this small, wild universe.

